  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "1.5.0: A listing reports a recycled directory's RecycleBinItem.Size as the total length of the files beneath it, at every depth; GetItemsOptions.MeasureDirectorySizes, on by default, turns that walk off. A directory that is a link, and a link inside one, contribute nothing to it, and a link's target is not walked. EmptyAsync's BytesFreed now includes the bytes beneath a recycled directory. Recycling an item whose original name has no extension no longer fails on a bin that already holds three such entries\n            1.4.0: A path reached through a junction or symbolic link is recycled into the Recycle Bin of the volume that holds it, and its record keeps the caller's path; a path that is itself a link goes to its own volume's bin\n            1.3.0: One failure stage enumeration per operation — RecycleFailureStage is gone, replaced by ListFailureStage, EmptyFailureStage, RecyclePathFailureStage and RestoreFailureStage, and RecycleFailure is now RecycleFailure<TStage>, so a stage an operation cannot produce will not compile against it. An entry an empty could not remove reports EmptyFailureStage.EntryNotRemoved rather than sharing Enumeration with a walk that failed. Breaking: the stage values are renumbered, so an int persisted against RecycleFailureStage no longer names a member\n            1.2.0: Progress reporting on RecycleAsync, RestoreAsync and EmptyAsync — each settings type carries OnItemProcessed, OnProgressFailed and WaitForProgressCallbacks, and each handler is given a RecycleProgress, a RestoreProgress or an EmptyProgress. EmptyAsync gains an overload taking EmptyOptions\n            1.1.0: Support for the first version of the Recycle Bin index file, on machines whose Windows reads that one rather than the second\n            1.0.0: First release",
